# Strict closed-hull coverage for rectilinear scaffold patches

The current cellwise coverage argument can be sharpened at the boundary. Let a
coordinate cell have side gaps a,b at least 1 in two unit directions making an
angle of 60 degrees, and put

    sigma(a,b) = a^2 - a*b + b^2.

Cut the cell along its short diagonal. Each resulting triangle has side lengths
a, b, and sqrt(sigma), with included angle 60 degrees. If sigma(a,b) < 3,
then a < 2, b < 2, and sqrt(sigma) < sqrt(3).

The covering radius of a triangle by open unit disks centered at its three
vertices is strictly below one in this situation. In the acute case it is at
most the circumradius

    sqrt(sigma) / sqrt(3) < 1;

in the right or obtuse case it is at most half the longest side, which is also
strictly below one. The inequality is strict, so it covers the closed triangle,
not only its interior. The two triangles cover the closed cell.

Therefore, for a nonuniform product patch with every cell satisfying
sigma(a,b) < 3, the closed hull of the patch is contained in the union of open
unit disks centered at the patch points.

This removes a boundary ambiguity in the source-side exclusion: if a unit-separated
set F contains P, then no point of F minus P can lie anywhere in the closed product
hull, including a cell edge or a hull edge. Any remaining source point is strictly
outside that hull. A lattice replacement site whose distance from the hull boundary
is at least one is consequently separated from all such exterior points.

The executable certificate is research/strict_hull_cover_certificate.py, with exact
Fraction regressions in research/test_strict_hull_cover_certificate.py. This is a
quantifier and boundary-control improvement to the rectilinear replacement route; it
does not produce the missing coherent product patch inside the actual scaffold, so
the Target A Schur/Hall endpoint remains open.
